@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the second person who stays outside the state, examined
#
# The statement at pmc:TheOneWhoStaysOutsideTheState named in its own
# falsification note the corpora to ask first: the solitary vigil and the fast,
# the Lakota hanblapi, the incubation literature and the desert material, all
# in the holding and none asked the question. This protocol asks them, adds the
# two transmissions of this holding that have since been read with the question
# in view — the Boran manual of 2026-09-26 and the Hasidic manual of
# 2026-10-15 — and one place of a second Hasidic work read for the purpose.
#
# It was devised against the claim and not found by accident; the Hasidic case
# came from the harvest of the same day, but the question was put to it at the
# reading and not afterwards.
#

pmp:OneWhoStaysOutsideExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether a transmission that takes its practitioner into a state he cannot check names a second person who stays outside it"@en, "Ob eine Überlieferung, die ihren Übenden in einen Zustand führt, den er nicht prüfen kann, eine zweite Person nennt, die außerhalb bleibt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the two-clause claim that a transmission whose practitioner enters, during the operation, a condition in which he can no longer check it names a second person who stays outside that condition, makes the outcome depend on him and states that without him the operation does not come off; and that a transmission binding the effect to something the operator checks on himself names no such person and may require solitude. The population is every node in the compiled holding that prescribes solitude, seclusion, sleep, vigil or a waiting for a vision, together with every node that names a person present at such an operation; drawn by a whole-text scan of traditions/ for alone, solitude, solitary, seclusion, companion, sleep, dream, vigil, watch and attendant, and read at the node. One place outside the holding was read for it at the wording: Keter Shem Tov, part 1, section 184 in the printing Zolkiew 1794-95."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheOneWhoStaysOutsideTheState ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "For the first clause: a transmission that takes its practitioner into a condition in which he cannot check the operation and names no second person, or names one who does not stay outside that condition, or names one on whom the outcome does not depend. Met three times, in three different ways, and the clause does not survive as worded. For the second clause: a transmission that binds the effect to something the practitioner checks on himself and yet requires a second person for the outcome. Not met; the one new case on this side confirms it and widens what counts as self-checking."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched: the compiled holding as of 2026-10-15, by the scan described in the definition, with every candidate read at its node; and, outside the holding, Tzava'at ha-Rivash read complete in the Hebrew (Warsaw 1913) and Keter Shem Tov read at part 1, section 184 (Zolkiew 1794-95, as given in the Sefaria library). Found against the first clause: the dream divination of the Neoplatonic file, the Hasidic seclusion of two in one room, and the Lakota quest. Found for it: the Mandaean week without sleep and the Boran lineage as reported in 1916. Not searched: the desert material, because the holding's two Byzantine nodes carry no passage on solitude and name no edition (prima-materia#45 at that file), so there was nothing to read at the node; and the incubation literature beyond what the Neoplatonic file holds, because the holding has no temple-incubation source of its own."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-15" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-10-15" ;
    skos:note "First deviation: no second person. The Neoplatonic file holds dream divination as the deliberate use of dreams, available to anyone and requiring no instrument beyond the sleeper, and states that this is where it differs from the incubation cult: no temple, no priest, no fee (pmp:DreamDivination). Sleep is the clearest case of a condition in which the practitioner cannot check the operation, and the transmission names nobody. The condition it does name is on the sleeper's own state beforehand, the purity of the vehicle (pmc:DreamDivinationPresupposesPurityOfTheVehicle). The case is weak in one respect that is the holding's and not this protocol's: the node does not name the edition read, and the passage was not reopened here."@en ;
    skos:note "Second deviation: a second person who does not stay outside. Tzava'at ha-Rivash, section 63, requires a companion for seclusion because one man alone is in danger, and places the two in one room, each secluding himself on his own with the Creator (pmc:HasidismHitbodedutYechidiSakana). The second person is required, but he enters the same condition, is given no office — not to watch, not to wake, not to answer — and what his absence costs is stated as danger to the practitioner and not as failure of the operation. The same section relaxes the rule when the practitioner is already attached: he can then seclude himself even in a house full of people. That seclusion is a condition the practitioner cannot check is the same work's own description of the state it aims at: in prayer one is to come to the level where one does not know or feel at all whether one is in this world (sections 62 and 97). The statement predicts the person and gets his position and his function wrong."@en ;
    skos:note "Third deviation: a second person placed after the state and not beside it. The Lakota quest sends the seeker alone to a high place where others are not likely to intrude, has him wait awake or asleep until the communication comes or until he is so exhausted that he cannot wait longer without danger of losing his life, and makes the meaning of what he received depend on a shaman whom he is to consult afterwards (pmp:Hanblapi, pmc:ProvisionForTheQuestThatYieldsNothing, pmc:CommunicationMustBeInterpretedByAShaman). There is an outside person and the outcome depends on him, but he is not present during the condition, and the stopping point is set by the practitioner's own judgement of his exhaustion."@en ;
    skos:note "For the first clause, two cases, each with a shift. The Mandaean candidate for the priesthood is kept from sleep for a week by a household beating drums and entertaining guests all night, because what sleep may bring would cost the consecration (pmc:MandaeanVigiliaSeptemDierum): the second persons stay outside and the outcome depends on them, but their office is to keep the practitioner out of the uncheckable condition rather than to watch him in it. The Boran lineage, as a Sinhala informant reported it in 1916, held that the practice must not be given by a guru who cannot see clairvoyantly what each meditation does to the pupil, and named a pupil who went mad and died (pmc:BoranSisyaUmmattako): a watcher outside the state, and a consequence without him, which is the first clause exactly — in the lineage's report and not in its manual, which sends the one second person it names away before the first station (pmc:BoranOkasaBhante)."@en ;
    skos:note "For the second clause, one new case and it widens the clause. Keter Shem Tov, part 1, section 184, read at the wording: in prayer one is to feel oneself in the world of making, then of formation, then of creation, until one feels oneself risen to the world of emanation, walking in thought from room to room; one is to take care not to fall from that height but to hold oneself there with all one's strength, as with bit and bridle; and when one wants to do this for attachment outside prayer, there must be no person in the house, for even the chirping of birds can undo it, and so can another man's thought. Solitude is required where the practitioner is told to monitor and hold his own state, which is the second clause; but what he checks is a felt position and not a wording, a count or an hour, so the clause's list was too narrow. The same circle, in Tzava'at ha-Rivash, requires the companion that this work forbids, and the two works differ on the very point the statement turns on."@en ;
    skos:note "What the deviations cost the statement, stated exactly. The second clause stands and gains a case and a wider class of self-check. The first clause does not stand as a universal. Of the five transmissions in the population that take a practitioner into a condition he cannot check, one names nobody, one names a second person inside the condition and for safety, one names a second person after it and for interpretation, one names second persons who keep him out of it, and one — in a report of its lineage and not in its text — names the watcher the statement describes. The three original witnesses, the seiðr, the Chukchee shaman and the Fourth Way, remain the only ones in the holding that state the clause as they were read. The rewriting made at the node follows from this: the first clause is made conditional on the transmission stating that the operation fails without a second person, and predicts only that such a person stays outside; the other positions found here are recorded as observed and not predicted. Narrowed so, the clause can still fail: by a transmission that says the operation does not come off without a second person who enters the same condition."@en ;
    skos:note "What a later run should do. The deciding property the statement proposed — whether the operator can check himself — still separates the two clauses and was not refuted; what failed is the prediction of one shape for the second person. The cheapest next question is what the stated consequence of the second person's absence follows. In the three original witnesses it is the failure of the operation; in the Hasidic seclusion and in the Boran lineage it is harm to the practitioner — danger, madness and death — and these two place the second person differently, inside the condition and outside it. So the consequence does not follow the position, and a reformulation on that axis would fail on the cases already read. What may decide it is whether the second person is given an office: the three witnesses and the Boran guru each have one, sing, answer, wake or watch; the Hasidic companion has none. The population for that question is every transmission in the holding that names a danger of practising alone, and it has not been drawn."@en .
